   "start_page": 5,
   "summary": "Ambassador Grew transmits Report No. 148 detailing conditions in Japan during January 1939, accompanied by monthly schedules of despatches and telegrams sent to Washington. Domestically, the report examines the resignation of Prime Minister Konoye and the installation of Baron Hiranuma's Cabinet, growing state regimentation under the National General Mobilization Law, and Diet debates over the 1939–40 budget. In foreign affairs, Japan maintained a firm stance against parallel American, British, and French notes upholding the Nine-Power Treaty, while disputes persisted over the Canton Customs, Yangtze River shipping, and Far Eastern Soviet fisheries. The report also chronicles Japanese military operations and aerial bombardments in China affecting foreign properties, Hungary's recognition of Manchukuo, Japanese emigration to Brazil, and the French refusal of agrément for Ambassador-designate Masayuki Tani.",
